ggml-cpu(x86): byte-wise prefetch address, and a type clang accepts on MSVC targets

The four _mm_prefetch calls in ggml/src/ggml-cpu/arch/x86/quants.c (the SSSE3-without-AVX
branch of ggml_vec_dot_q4_0_q8_0) carry two independent defects. Both are fixed by routing the
address through one new static inline helper, ggml/src/ggml-cpu/arch/x86/prefetch.h; the call
sites change from a raw expression to ggml_prefetch_at(&x[ib], sizeof(block_q4_0)) and nothing
else moves. The helper is header-only and included relatively, so no CMake change is needed.

1. Type -- this is what makes the build fail. MSVC declares the intrinsic as
   `void _mm_prefetch(char const *, int)`. Clang's own xmmintrin.h defines a macro that casts to
   `const void *`, but it sits behind `#ifndef _MSC_VER`: on a *-windows-msvc target clang leaves
   the declaration to Microsoft. Passing `const block_q4_0 *` is then
   -Wincompatible-pointer-types, which clang 16 and later report as an error by default.
   Measured on Windows 11 with MSVC 14.44.35207 headers: plain clang (the GNU driver, which
   cmake/x64-windows-llvm.cmake selects) fails with 4 errors at -msse4.2, both with LLVM 20.1.8
   and 23.1.3, and builds at -msse4.2 -mavx because the AVX branch is taken instead. clang-cl
   and MSVC only warn; clang on a GNU target never sees it. The practical consequence is that
   -DGGML_CPU_ALL_VARIANTS=ON cannot be built with plain clang on Windows, because the x64 and
   sse42 variants are exactly the ones that compile this branch.

2. Arithmetic -- wrong on every platform, including where the call compiles.
   `&x[ib] + sizeof(block_q4_0)` adds sizeof() *elements*, not bytes. With the sizes that
   ggml-common.h pins by static_assert (block_q4_0 == 18, block_q8_0 == 34) the prefetch targets
   +324 and +1156 bytes instead of +18 and +34, and the "+ 2 *" pair targets +648 instead of
   +36 -- 18 and 34 times too far. The call sites plainly intend "the next block" and "two blocks
   ahead", which is what byte-wise arithmetic gives. Measured: no throughput difference on a
   Ryzen 7 5800H forced onto the sse42 kernel with a 0.6B Q4_0 model (pp512 101.3 vs 100.9,
   tg128 37.3 vs 37.0, both inside the other's error bars), so this is a correctness fix, not a
   performance one; a genuine SSE4.2-era machine was not available to measure.

Upstream-submittable, and intended to be submitted: the fix is confined to one new header plus
four call sites and changes no behaviour beyond the prefetch address. Open question for the
upstream report: upstream's own windows-cpu release job builds plain clang with
GGML_CPU_ALL_VARIANTS=ON and no suppressing flag, and ships ggml-cpu-sse42.dll, so their
toolchain evidently does not hit (1) -- most likely a different MSVC header version on the
windows-2025-vs2026 runner.

Runnable guard: src/test/cpp/test_prefetch.cpp drives ggml_prefetch_at against the real
block_q4_0 / block_q8_0 types and asserts the offsets are byte-wise. That test is red if this
patch is dropped, and -- the case that matters -- also red if the cast is written around the
whole expression, `(const char *)(&x[ib] + sizeof(block_q4_0))`, which fixes (1) and silently
keeps (2).

On a bump, check whether upstream fixed this themselves:
`git grep -n _mm_prefetch ggml/src/ggml-cpu/arch/x86/quants.c` in the new tag. If the call sites
already cast before the addition, DROP this patch rather than refreshing it.

diff --git a/ggml/src/ggml-cpu/arch/x86/prefetch.h b/ggml/src/ggml-cpu/arch/x86/prefetch.h
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..9be944e
--- /dev/null
+++ b/ggml/src/ggml-cpu/arch/x86/prefetch.h
@@ -0,0 +1,25 @@
+#pragma once
+
+#include <stddef.h>
+
+// Byte-wise address arithmetic for _mm_prefetch.
+//
+// Two reasons this helper exists instead of casting at the call site:
+//
+// 1. Type. MSVC declares the intrinsic as `void _mm_prefetch(char const *, int)`
+//    (VC\Tools\MSVC\<ver>\include\xmmintrin.h). Clang's own header defines a macro that casts
+//    to `const void *`, but only `#ifndef _MSC_VER` -- on a *-windows-msvc target clang leaves
+//    the declaration to Microsoft. Passing a typed pointer there is -Wincompatible-pointer-types,
+//    which clang 16 and later report as an error by default, so plain clang (the GNU driver, as
+//    used by cmake/x64-windows-llvm.cmake) cannot compile such a call. MSVC and clang-cl only
+//    warn, and clang on a GNU target never sees it because its macro casts first.
+//
+// 2. Arithmetic. `&x[ib] + sizeof(block_q4_0)` adds sizeof() *elements*, not bytes: with
+//    sizeof(block_q4_0) == 18 that is 324 bytes ahead instead of the next block, and
+//    sizeof(block_q8_0) == 34 gives 1156 instead of 34. Casting to `const char *` first -- what
+//    this helper does -- is what makes the offset byte-wise, which is plainly what the call
+//    sites intend ("the next block", "two blocks ahead"). This half is wrong on every platform,
+//    including the ones where the call compiles.
+static inline const char * ggml_prefetch_at(const void * base, size_t byte_offset) {
+    return (const char *) base + byte_offset;
+}
diff --git a/ggml/src/ggml-cpu/arch/x86/quants.c b/ggml/src/ggml-cpu/arch/x86/quants.c
index ea54cfe..dea0952 100644
--- a/ggml/src/ggml-cpu/arch/x86/quants.c
+++ b/ggml/src/ggml-cpu/arch/x86/quants.c
@@ -7,6 +7,7 @@
 
